<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baltic Landeswehr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Baltische Landeswehr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baltic Landeswehr was a volunteer force formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> in late 1918, mainly from Baltic German nobles and townspeople, with Latvian and Russian units. Fighting alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">German Freikorps</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, it recaptured Riga in May 1919. It was defeated by Estonian and Latvian forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a> in June 1919 and was then reorganised under British supervision as part of the Latvian army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baltic-landeswehr.jpg" alt="Baltic Landeswehr" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Graphic made by Carport · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Baltic_Germans.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the armistice of 11 November 1918 the Inter-Allied Commission of Control insisted that German troops stay in the Baltic to keep out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. As the Soviet offensive approached, the Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> signed an agreement with August Winnig, the German representative in the Baltics, on 7 December 1918, authorising land defence forces. A second agreement on 29 December promised full Latvian citizenship to foreign soldiers who fought for Latvia's freedom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was to supply arms and uniforms and the Latvian government food. The force grew out of Baltic German formations tied to the Couronian and Livonian nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By late February 1919 only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> and its surroundings were still in German and Latvian hands. In February and March the Landeswehr defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ventspils">Ventspils</a> and advanced on Riga. The killing of three of its men led to the coup of 16 April 1919, which proclaimed a government under the Lutheran clergyman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrievs-niedra">Andrievs Niedra</a>. Riga was taken on 22 May, when Baron Hans von Manteuffel-Szoege entered with a small detachment and was killed leading his men.</p><p>In June, General Rüdiger von der Goltz, commander of the German VI Reserve Corps to which the Landeswehr was subordinated, sent his troops north against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> rather than east against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. The attack around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a> began on 19 June, and the Estonian 3rd Division under Ernst Põdder, with the Latvian 2nd Cēsis regiment, defeated it; on 23 June the Germans retreated towards Riga.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> imposed a ceasefire and placed the Landeswehr under British authority. Lieutenant-Colonel Harold Alexander, who took command in mid-July 1919, dismissed men born within Imperial Germany; they joined the Deutsche Legion, which served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a>'s West Russian Volunteer Army until its defeat in November 1919. The purged units helped Latvian and Polish armies drive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> in January 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its commanders were Major Emil von Scheibler until 6 February 1919, then Major Alfred Fletcher until 3 July and Alexander after him. In May 1919 its order of battle held three Baltic German battalions, a Russian detachment under Prince Anatol Lieven, a Latvian brigade under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janis-balodis">Jānis Balodis</a>, cavalry and artillery. Its men wore shoulder straps piped in light blue and white, the Baltic colours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baltische Landeswehr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…aided by Finnish volunteers and a British naval squadron, drove the invaders out by early 1919, defeated the <b>Baltic German Landeswehr</b> at the Battle of Cēsis in June 1919, and fought on until Soviet Russia recognised Estonian independence in perpetuity by the Treaty of Tartu on 2 February…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Latvian and Estonian troops defeated the German <b>Landeswehr</b> at the Battle of Cēsis in June 1919, the national government returned to Riga, and the attack of Pavel Bermondt-Avalov's West Russian Volunteer Army on the capital was repulsed in November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>German Freikorps and the <b>Baltic Landeswehr</b>, with Latvian national units, recaptured Riga on 22 May 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Baltic German volunteers later fought in the <b>Landeswehr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-baltic-duchy">United Baltic Duchy</a></li></ul></section>
